          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "**Coronary artery disease (CAD)** is a build-up of fatty plaque inside the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. The plaque forms over many years. It can narrow the arteries, which limits blood flow when the heart works harder and often causes predictable chest discomfort during activity. It can also crack, which can trigger a sudden clot and a heart attack, even at a spot that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms. (Source: *Coronary artery disease*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**How it is classified:** In the ICD-10-CM coding system, CAD falls in the range I20 to I25, under ischemic heart disease. This range runs from angina (chest pain from reduced blood flow) through heart attack to the long-term atherosclerotic disease that follows. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 9*.) Codes are used for records and billing. They do not describe how severe your condition is.\n\n**Ongoing care:** Guidance on long-term management, called secondary prevention, uses a combination of measures rather than one treatment. It includes long-term antiplatelet medicine and high-intensity statin therapy, which a clinician decides on for each person. The guidance says the non-drug parts are often the ones people drop, so they matter too. My search did not return the specific lifestyle recommendations, so I cannot list them here. (Source: *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis*.)\n\n**Get emergency help now** if you have chest pain or pressure that does not settle, especially with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Call emergency services rather than driving yourself. A false alarm is a good outcome, and waiting can cause lasting harm. (Source: *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*.)\n\nIf you tell me which symptoms you have, or which questions you want to explore, I can help you understand what the guidance says and what to raise with your clinician. Please bear in mind that I cannot assess your own situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
